course: dictionary
lesson: 8909
title: Shyly: how someone does something
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Adverbs tell us how, when or where something happens. Shyly tells us how someone does an action. It means in a nervous, quiet way, because you feel uncomfortable with other people or with something new.
explain: «The field-mouse asked to recite and giggled shyly.» Look at the words around shyly to check its meaning. The mouse is nervous about reciting, and his giggle is small and unsure. The sentence after the dash says his stage-fright was too strong to overcome, which fits a shy, nervous feeling.
explain: Always ask: how did they do it? Then try your meaning in the sentence. «She said shyly, "Hello."» If you swap in nervously and quietly, the sentence still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Shyly is an adverb, and it describes how a verb such as smiled, said or looked is done.
fact: «Shyly» means in a nervous, quiet way, because you feel uncomfortable with other people or new things.
fact: Its other forms are more shyly and most shyly.
fact: Common phrases with shyly are smiled shyly, said shyly and looked away shyly.
fact: Context helps: «giggled shyly» tells you the giggle was nervous, not loud or happy.
exercise: «The field-mouse asked to recite and giggled shyly.» What does shyly tell us about the way the mouse giggled? | In a fast, angry way | In a loud, happy way | In a bored, careless way | *In a nervous, quiet way
exercise: Maya «smiled shyly» at the new girl in class. What does shyly show about Maya? | *She felt nervous and unsure | She was telling a joke | She was in a hurry | She was very cross
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as shyly? | loudly | rudely | *nervously | bravely
test: Ella looked up ___ and hid behind her book. | proudly | quickly | *shyly | angrily
test: Sam spoke ___ than his sister, who loves to shout. | shyest | *more shyly | loudly | shy
exam: «He stood at the door and waved shyly.» Why did he wave shyly? | He was very angry with his friends | He was dancing and singing | *He felt nervous about joining the new group | He was late for school
